Chemiresistive Detection of Gaseous Hydrocarbons and Interrogation of Charge Transport in Cu[Ni(2,3- pyrazinedithiolate)2] by Gas Adsorption
## INTRODUCTION
The quantification of gas mixture composition is a critical capability for any industry that requires atmospheric control or employs reactive gases, including the chemical refinement, semiconductor, and medical industries.1 Gas sensors are typically constructed from three basic components: a sensing material that undergoes a change in physical properties upon interaction with analyte, a complementary transducer, and the electronics required to measure the electrical response of the transducer.2 Sensors based on chemiresistivity—a change in electrical conductance upon interaction with a target compound—are particularly attractive, as they eliminate the need for an expensive transducer, such as a piezoelectric sensor, photometer, or spectrometer. However, suitable chemiresistive materials do not exist for many environments and gas compositions. The development of new chemiresistive gas sensors may therefore enhance control over existing processes and environments and promote greater adoption of sensors in industry, as a result of anticipated cost reductions.
been used in many applications, including hydrogen–carbon monoxide and hydrocarbon-based gas mixtures. However, they remain fundamentally limited by the high operating temperatures required, low analyte selectivity in certain instances, and a lack of rational design methodologies for tailoring sensor materials to specific gas mixtures and operating conditions.
One possible materials solution to the challenge of designing improved chemiresistive sensors would be to replace the metal oxide sensor component with a metal–organic framework—a porous, crystalline solid composed of metal nodes connected via organic linkers. The vast structural diversity and modular constitution of metal–organic frameworks are distinct advantages compared to metal oxides, and are properties largely responsible for the widespread interest in these materials for other applications, including gas storage and catalysis.6 For example, the hybrid nature of metal–organic frameworks may support the amalgamation of disparate properties that rarely coexist—such as crystallinity, microporosity, and conductivity—to yield new materials with synergistic properties.7-10 Recent advances in the chemistry of electronically conductive metal–organic frameworks11,12 have introduced the prospect of coupling charge transport and selective host guest interactions. Fundamentally, highly porous materials with
crystallographically-defined surfaces may also provide a unique testing ground for exploring how surface–adsorbate interactions modulate electronic transport—given that these surface interactions are expressed as changes in the bulk chemical structure. Further developing synthetic control over charge mobility in porous crystals may also promote the use of such materials in new technologies surrounding energy storage,13-15 electrocatalysis,16-20 and electronic devices.21-23
Previous efforts towards the synthesis and integration of metal–organic frameworks into sensor devices have been recently reviewed.24,25 Notably, there have been a number of breakthroughs with regard to chemiresistive applications of metal–organic frameworks in the presence of either redox active gases26  or adsorbed solvents and volatile organic guest species.27-30 For example, a chemiresistive sensor array constructed from two-dimensional triphenylene-based $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ frameworks has been shown to be capable of classifying a diverse range of volatile organic compounds based on chemical functional group.29 In this case, the effect of guest binding on conductivity was found to be rather complex and somewhat unpredictable, opening up an enticing opportunity to explore the effects of adsorption on conductivity in greater detail.
Motivated by this study, we chose to investigate the previously reported three-dimensional conductive framework material $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ (pdt2–=pyrazine-2,3-dithiolate; Figure 1) as a case study for chemiresistivity in metal–organic frameworks.31 This particular phase was an early example of a permanently porous, conductive metal–organic framework—in contrast to the isostructural compound $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{32}$ —and remains a rare case of a metal–organic framework exhibiting both a measurable room-temperature conductivity $(10^{-8}\mathrm{S/cm})$ and permanent porosity. Herein, we examine the electrical conductance of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ as a function of absolute pressure in the presence of selected light hydrocarbons. Changes in the conductance of the material in response to pure component adsorption can be directly correlated to gas adsorption measurements collected on the same equipment under identical conditions. Accordingly, it is possible to determine accurate conductivity-composition profiles in order to elucidate how specific adsorbates modulate conductivity in this rare example of a porous conductor.
## EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURES
General procedures and measurements. The synthesis of $\mathrm{{Na}}_{2}[\mathrm{{Ni}(p d t)_{2}]}$ was carried out according to the literature procedure.31 Diffraction data were collected using a Bruker AXS D8 Advance diffractometer with the generator set at 40 $\mathbf{kV}$ and $40\mathrm{mA}$ , and samples were loaded onto zero-background sample holders. Elemental analyses were obtained from the Microanalytical Laboratory at the University of California, Berkeley.
Modified Synthesis of $\mathbf{Cu}[\mathbf{Ni}(\mathbf{pdt})_{2}]$ . The synthesis of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ was adapted from a previous report.31 A flask was charged with $270~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetonitrile and sparged with dry ${\bf N}_{2}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Next, $\mathrm{{Na}}_{2}[\mathrm{{Ni}(p d t)_{2}]}$ $\left(0.630\mathrm{g},1.62\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ was added to the solvent and the solution was stirred. A separate flask was charged with $75~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetonitrile, sparged with dry ${\bf N}_{2}$ for 30 min, and $\mathrm{CuI}$ $\mathrm{~I~}(0.309\mathrm{~g},1.62\mathrm{~mmol})$ was added. While under a positive atmosphere of $\Nu_{2}$ , the CuI solution was slowly added to the solution of $\mathrm{{Na}}_{2}[\mathrm{{Ni}(p d t)_{2}]}$ via a syringe, which resulted in the rapid precipitation of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ as a black solid. The solid was filtered on $0.22\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ nylon filter paper in air and activated overnight at $90\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under high vacuum on a Micromeritics ASAP 2420 instrument equipped with a turbo pump, yielding $0.453\textrm{g}(69\%)$ of the product as a dark red microcrystalline solid. The X-ray powder diffraction pattern for the activated material is shown in Figure S1 and the gas adsorption isotherm used to calculate the Langmuir surface area is provided in Figure S2. Elemental Analysis for $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ : calculated., C $23.63\%$ , $H0.99\%$ , N $13.78\%$ , $\mathrm{~S~}31.54\%$ ; found, $=22.92\%$ , H $1.05\%$ , $\mathrm{~N~}13.74\%$ , $\mathrm{~S~}30.43\%$ .
Gas Adsorption Measurements. Gas adsorption data were measured between 0 and 1.1 bar using a Micromeritics 3-Flex instrument. Samples of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ powder were loaded into a pre-weighed tube, and heated at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. The mass of the activated sample was used as the basis for the adsorption measurements. After an adsorption isotherm was measured, the sample was reactivated at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before measuring a subsequent adsorption isotherm.
Ac Impedance Measurements. In an Ar-filled glovebox, activated $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ was pressed into a pellet inside a 2- contact PEEK screw cell, as previously reported.33 The ac conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ was determined using a Bio-Logic VMP-3 multipotentiostat equipped with an impedance analyzer operating between the frequencies 1 MHz and $0.01\mathrm{Hz}$ at a sinus amplitude of $100\mathrm{mV}$ and dc bias voltage of $0\mathrm{mV}$ (Figure S3). The observed spectrum was ohmic with dc bias voltage (Figure S4), linear with sinus amplitude (Figure S5), time invariant (Figure S5), and matched closely to what would be expected for a single Debye-type relaxation process with a single time constant (Figures S3, S6–S8).34,35
Time Resolved Conductivity Measurements upon Gas Adsorption. Conductivity and gas adsorption data were measured simultaneously as a function of time using a custombuilt conductivity cell (Figure 2). Printed circuit board arrays were designed using the free computer-aided design software fabrication. The arrays consisted of sets of two via and two 1- oz copper rails separated by a $0.152\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ gap printed onto an FR4 base. A segment of $\%$ -inch stainless steel tubing was cut, deburred, and fitted with Swagelok ferrules. Individual boards were cut to fit within the $\%$ -inch tubing and silver coated copper wires were soldered into place. The board was then threaded through the pre-cut tubing, and the bottom two-thirds of the tube was filled with a solventless high-vacuum epoxy (TorrSeal $\textsuperscript{\textregistered}$ or Hysol 1C) such that the board was fully exposed above the end of the tube (Figure 2). The gas-tight connection to the Micromeritics 3-Flex instrument consisted of a $\%$ -inch union, a reducing union, and a two-way Swagelok ball valve for air-free sample transfers.
The circuit board and adapter were transferred to an Ar-filled glovebox, and a thin pellet $(\sim50~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ powder was formed between the two copper rails using a mechanical press. The circuit board was sealed in the measurement cell in the Ar-filled glovebox and the entire apparatus was then connected to the analysis port on the gas sorption analyzer. After multiple cycles between 1 bar and ${<}1~\upmu\mathrm{bar}$ of helium, the cell was left to stabilize under vacuum. In a typical measurement, currents of $1{-}10\upmu\mathrm{A}$ were applied (depending of the resistance of the sample) and the voltage response measured with $0.5\mathrm{~s~}$ time resolution. An exemplary time dependent data set is given in Figure S9, showing clearly-resolved steps between pressure points. The standard deviation of the baseline noise was ${\sim}0.7\mathrm{mV}.$ . Signal to noise ratios were ${\sim}3{:}1$ for the first pressure point in Figure S10 and greater than 10:1 for all subsequent pressure points. Rate of adsorption data were collected from the 3-Flex gas adsorption analyzer, using the PuTTY client to access the data transmitted from the instrument to the computer with $0.5\mathrm{~s~}$ time resolution.
Crystal Structure Determinations. Powder X-ray diffraction data for $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ was collected on Beamline 17- BM-B at the Advanced Photon Source at Argonne National Laboratory. Approximately $3\mathrm{mg}$ of fully desolvated framework was loaded into a $1.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ borosilicate capillary inside a $\mathrm{N}_{2}.$ filled glovebox. Each capillary was then attached to a custom designed gas-dosing cell equipped with a gas valve and transferred to the goniometer head. All adsorbed ${\bf N}_{2}$ was then removed by evacuating in situ using a turbomolecular pump. An Oxford Cryosystems Cryostream 800 was used to hold the temperature of the sample at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Scattered intensity was measured by a PerkinElmer $\upalpha$ -Si flat panel detector.
Computational Methods. The parallel version of the CRYSTAL17 suite of programs was used for all calculations.36,37 The structure of activated $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ , as well as the material containing either two $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ molecules per unit cell was optimized while keeping the lattice parameters fixed at their experimental values. This prevented the unit cell from becoming too compressed due to the overestimation of dispersive interactions. Frequency calculations were carried out for the optimized structures to confirm that they were in a local minimum. The original tetragonal symmetry was removed in order to define the spin on each metal center in the unit cell.
The unrestricted PBE038-D3(BJ)38,39 hybrid density functional was employed, with the pob_TZVP basis-set,40 which consists of a Gaussian-type, all-electron triple- $\zeta$ basis set derived from the popular def2-TZVP,41 the former being devised especially for solid-state calculations. Grimme’s -D3 a posteriori correction scheme39 for dispersive interactions was coupled with the Becke-Johnson’s (BJ) damping function.42
A very fine DFT grid (keyword XXLGRID37) was utilized whereas, based on total energy convergence, a Pack-Monkhorst grid of $112\ k$ -points (keyword SHRINK 6 6) within the first Brillouin zone was chosen. The tolerance thresholds on bielectronic Coulomb and exchange integrals (see keyword TOLINTEG), were set to $10^{-12}10^{-12}10^{-12}10^{-14}10^{-22}$ . The DIIS acceleration algorithm43 was used throughout. Starting guesses of the density matrix for different spin multiplets were originated by assigning the spin to each metal center with the keyword ATOMSPIN, at cycle 0 of the self-consistent procedure (SCF). No locking of unpaired electrons—that is, no locking to a particular spin configuration guess—was imposed in the successive cycles of the SCF.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The synthesis of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ was performed via a slight modification of the previously reported procedure30 to afford a dark red crystalline powder with a nearly identical Langmuir surface area of $426~\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . The structure of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ (Figure1) has been previously reported and consists of sulfurcoordinated square planar nickel(II) pyrazine-2,3-dithiolate units linked by nitrogen-coordinated square planar copper(II)
centers in a three-dimensional lattice. The resulting framework features
square channels with edge dimensions defined by the $\mathrm{Cu}^{\cdot\cdot\cdot}\mathrm{Cu}$ separation of 6.8132(10) Å and walls that are lined by the $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{2-}$ complexes. While the structure is anisotropic, significant conductivity along any of the three crystallographic axes may be expected, in view of the similarity in metal coordination environments and connectivity.
Hopping Charge Transport in $\mathbf{Cu}[\mathbf{Ni}(\mathbf{pdt})_{2}]$ . Two-contact, pressed pellet conductivity measurements carried out on guestfree $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ revealed a conductivity of $2.6~{\upmu\mathrm{S/cm}}$ , which notably is 260 times greater than the previously reported value.31 This difference is likely the result of improved sample purity, homogeneity, or desolvation conditions. We note that measured differences in conductivity of this magnitude are not unusual, even with relatively small differences in synthetic conditions.30,34 As $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ was originally reported to exhibit thermally-activated conductivity,30 we employed ac impedance spectroscopy to distinguish between bipolar- and hopping-type conduction mechanisms, which both display exponential temperature dependences that are identical in form.34 By this method, a single ohmic Debye-type relaxation mode was observed for $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ , with a single time constant (Figure S3).35 Thus, bulk conductivity in $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ possibly manifests as nearest neighbor charge hopping via small polarons localized on individual $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{2-}$ units,34 although the exact identity of the charge carriers could not be reliably determined experimentally or theoretically. Given that the observed conductivity is much higher than would be expected for a closed shell $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{2-}$ configuration, one possibility is that a small fraction of the $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{2-}$ units undergo transient oxidation along with concomitant reduction of neighboring $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions to $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ .32 However, it is also possible that the presence of small quantities of oxidized $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]^{2}$ –—undetectable by our analytical methods—are responsible for the observed conductivity. This hypothesis is aligned with previous photocurrent experiments suggesting a $p$ -type conduction mechanism.31
Initially, in order to examine the effect of guest adsorption on conductivity, $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ was exposed to a saturated atmosphere of acetonitrile (the synthesis solvent) vapor at room temperature for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ . Remarkably, following this exposure, the conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]{\cdot}x\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ decreased to 0.015 $\upmu\mathrm{S}/\upmathrm{c m}$ . This value agrees well with the originally reported conductivity for $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ of $0.01~\upmu\mathrm{S/cm}$ , and is nearly 200 times lower than the conductivity measured for the desolvated framework here.31 Reported changes in metal–organic framework conductivity upon exposure to volatile organic compounds are typically much smaller—on the order of ten percent28,29—although hydration of a conductive mixed-valence ruthenium metal-organic framework has been reported to result in changes in conductivity of several orders of magnitude.44 The rather high sensitivity of the $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ conductivity to a volatile organic compound encouraged us to examine the effect of other compounds that typically interact less strongly with porous materials—such as light hydrocarbons—and are therefore typically more difficult to detect and distinguish.1,45
For this purpose, we constructed a conductivity cell that could be attached to a Micromeritics 3-Flex gas adsorption analyzer (see Figure 2 for a simplified diagram). While this setup can support a number of sample geometries, given the relatively low conductivity of $\mathrm{{Cu}[N i(p d t)_{2}]}$ , a two-contact method was determined to be sufficient. A thin pellet of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ ${50}{-}100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ thick) was pressed between the two copper traces (spaced $152\upmu\mathrm{m}$ apart) on a printed circuit board. The measurement cell was then sealed in an air-tight Swagelok tube and attached to a gas adsorption analyzer.
Using this apparatus, the room-temperature conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ was initially measured in the presence of a variety of gases at 1 bar. The adsorption of nitrogen, helium, argon, or methane resulted in very small conductivity changes ${\sim}1\%$ or less). Given this small chemiresistive response, especially when compared to the saturated acetonitrile phase, the signal-to-noise level was too low to obtain accurate data for these gases. However, considering that $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ binds these gases only weakly and adsorbs only a small amount at room temperature, such a small change in conductivity is not unexpected.
Selective Hydrocarbon Adsorption in $\mathbf{Cu}[\mathbf{Ni(pdt)}_{2}]$ . The $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ framework does adsorb significant quantities of hydrocarbons larger than methane, specifically ethane, propane, acetylene, ethylene, propylene, and cis-2-butene. It should be noted that the gas adsorption and separation properties of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ in the presence of carbon dioxide, methane, acetylene, and ethylene have been recently reported.46 Relatedly, discrete nickel(II) dithiolene complexes have been shown to interact strongly through chemisorption with unsaturated hydrocarbons in solution.47,48 However, because the dithiolene units in $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ remain unoxidized, it is unlikely that a similar mechanism is operative here. The material displays the highest adsorption capacity for acetylene followed by ethylene and also displays the strongest affinity for these gases at low pressures, as indicated by the steep rise in their adsorption isotherms up to 0.1 bar (Figure 3a). In contrast, the larger olefins and paraffins all display qualitatively similar adsorption profiles, weaker binding and their adsorption saturates at approximately one molecule for every two formula units of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ . In the case of cis-2-butene, a slight deviation from Type I adsorption behavior occurs at high pressures, in the form of a slight upturn in the adsorption curve, and could possibly be ascribed to secondary adsorption occurring outside of the pores of $\mathrm{{Cu}[N i(p d t)_{2}]}$ .49 Each adsorption isotherm was modeled using the LangmuirFreundlich equation, and in the case of ethane, ethylene, propane, and propylene this model yielded fits in good agreement with the experimental data.50 For acetylene and cis2-butene, the adsorption isotherms were better fit with a dual site Langmuir-Freundlich (Table S1). With this set of probe molecules displaying a range of apparent binding strengths, we sought to investigate any corresponding differences in the conductance of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ upon gas loading.
Determination of Guest-Adsorbed Structures. Adsorption of the smallest two gases, acetylene and ethylene, was structurally characterized by powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (Figure 4). Upon incremental dosing from 20 to 1077 mbar of acetylene at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the powder diffraction pattern remained consistent with the space group of the degassed crystal, while significant changes in peak intensities were observed, suggestive of gas adsorption and crystallographically structured guest binding (Figures S11-S16 and Table S3). In comparison to the degassed structure (redetermined by X-ray diffraction here),31 at 291 mbar of acetylene, the Fourier difference map revealed a significant increase in electron density along the center of the pore axis (see the Supporting Information for details). The inclusion of acetylene within the lattice was modeled as two carbon atoms constrained to a distance of $1.20\textup{\AA}$ to yield the structure depicted in Figure 4a. The refined structure reveals acetylene to sit 3.4117(4) Å from the centroids of the four pyrazine rings on each side of the square channel. We note that very similar adsorption sites were recently crystallographically determined for acetylene in $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ and $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ .46
The adsorption of ethylene was similarly observed at various pressures (Figures S17-S21 and Table S4) and again excess electron density was observable in the Fourier difference map with respect to the degassed structure. The position of ethylene within the structure was modeled as two carbon atoms with a constrained bond length of $1.34\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . As shown in Figure 4b, ethylene appears similarly centered along the square channel axis, 3.4193(2) Å from the centroids of the pyrazine rings, with no observed reduction in symmetry with respect to $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ and only a very small lattice expansion of $0.3\%$ upon adsorption. Given the similarity in chemical structure, the larger adsorbates analyzed by gas adsorption likely prefer the same adsorption site within the framework, albeit with only about one-half of those sites occupied. The relatively strong adsorption of these two molecules, Figure 3a, is likely attributed to their ideal fit within the pore dimensions of the $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ lattice (Figure S22).46
Modulation of Conductivity upon Gas Adsorption. A series of dosing and evacuation cycles were initially carried out on a pressed pellet of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ with ethylene at pressures ranging from 0.5 to 8 mbar, resulting in transient changes in measured current at constant applied voltage (Figure S9). Significantly, each evacuation resulted in a return to the baseline current, indicating that this adsorbate-induced change in conductivity is reversible. During the dose-evacuation cycling, the conductance saturated after only ${\sim}10~\mathrm{s}$ , a relatively fast response, suggesting that the equilibration time for this change is on a time scale similar to gas adsorption and therefore that the two measurements may be conducted simultaneously. However, given the extremely small amount of material used in the conductivity measurements, the isotherms shown in Figure 3a were collected separately using a larger quantity of loose powder from the same synthesis batch obtained by methods established previously.51 This data was then used in subsequent quantitative analysis.
Room temperature, steady state conductivities of the phases $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]{\cdot}n{\mathrm{C}}_{x}\mathrm{H}_{y}$ were determined under constant current conditions as a function of pressure. As shown in Figure 3b, the change in conductance, $\Delta\upsigma$  was normalized to the initial baseline conductance, $\upsigma_{0}$ , which was measured prior to gas dosing at $1~\upmu\mathrm{bar}$ . Equilibration times for both adsorption and conductivity were established using pressure stabilization conditions preset by the gas adsorption analyzer. Figure S10 shows a typical voltage response data collected during the sequential gas dosing and equilibration procedure. The steadystate pressure dependence of the conductivity was fit using an equation identical in form to the Langmuir-Freundlich model used to fit the gas adsorption isotherms. While this model is tentatively empirical, the apparent goodness of fit and the qualitative similarity in curvature to the corresponding gas adsorption isotherms does suggest that conductivity depends linearly upon $n$ . We note that while a qualitative LangmuirFreundlich dependence on analyte concentration has been observed in other chemiresistive sensors,52 linear and logarithmic concentration dependences are also common.53
Noting the similarity in shape between the gas adsorption and conductivity isotherms, we now consider the three smallest adsorbates ethane, ethylene, and acetylene. The conductivity and adsorption curves for acetylene-dosed $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ both exhibit a steep rise at very low pressures before turning over to a gradual plateau at ${\sim}0.1$ bar. For pressures beyond 0.1 bar, a clear step in conductivity upon subsequent gas dosing was not observed, and therefore quantification of conductance at higher pressures was forgone. However, acetylene did show significant chemiresistivity at the lowest pressures for the adsorbates measured— $0.5\%$ at $72\ \upmu\mathrm{bar}$ . In contrast, conductance data for the ethylene and ethane isotherms could be collected up to 1
bar. Interestingly, for these three adsorbates it is clear that the magnitude of the chemiresistive effect does not depend on either the strength of guest binding or the total adsorption capacity. Indeed, at all pressures significantly more ethylene is adsorbed than ethane, yet the change in conductivity for ethane is greater than that of ethylene for pressures greater than 0.5 bar. This result suggests that differences in the chemical properties of the adsorbates—not observable by gas adsorption alone—are in part responsible for the modulation in electrical conductivity of the host lattice.
The absence of a clear correlation between adsorption capacity and the change in conductivity is better illustrated upon consideration of propylene and propane. Strikingly, the chemiresistive response of propylene- and propane-dosed $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ is nearly double that observed with acetylene and ethylene, despite the lower adsorption capacity and relatively weak adsorbate binding. In the case of the largest adsorbate cis2-butene, an enhancement in conductivity is observed over that measured with ethane and ethylene, although it is less than that determined for the C3-dosed framework at equivalent pressures. While this difference might be the result of reduced intracrystallite diffusion of cis-2-butene, the similar adsorption profiles of cis-2-butene, propylene, and propane suggest a different origin.
The calculations show that the projected density of states (Figure S25) has a nickel dithiolene contribution to the valence band edge and a primarily copper-based conduction band with a theoretical band gap of $2.1~\mathrm{eV}$ , in good agreement with the previously reported optical band gap of ${\sim}2\ \mathrm{eV}$ . There are no sizable differences in the electronic structure of bare $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ and the $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}].$ -adsorbate host-guest complexes (Figures S24-S33). As expected, the band structure at the frontier displays little dispersion $(<0.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ ; Figure S24) and is consistent with a charge hopping model for charge transport.
Conductivity–Composition Profiles. A more intuitive understanding of the observed changes in $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ conductivity was obtained by examining the variation of conductivity as a function of phase composition, rather than absolute pressure. Using the described isotherm fits, points of equivalent pressure in Figures 3a and 3b were calculated to determine the conductivity-composition relationships plotted in Figure 3c. For all gases except cis-2-butene, the conductivity of each hydrocarbon-loaded framework exhibits an approximately linear dependence on the equivalent of gas adsorbed. Here, we define the slope of each plot—the change in conductance per mole of gas adsorbed—as the sensitivity, $S_{;}$ , of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ to each adsorbate (Figures S36–S41 and Table S16). The linear regime of each plot was determined using the random sample consensus method (see the Supporting Information for details). From this method, we found that sensitivity increases in the order acetylene $\sim$ ethylene $<$ ethane $<$ propylene $\sim$ cis-2-butene $<$ propane. Interestingly, the conductivity is least sensitive to the quantity of acetylene adsorbed $(S=0.153(9)$ ), followed closely by ethylene $\mathit{S}=0.172(6)\$ ), a surprising result given that acetylene interacts most strongly with the host lattice.
Excluding cis-2-butene, the most immediately apparent trend is that the sensitivity increases with increasing molecular size (Table 1). When a hydrocarbon gas is adsorbed in $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ , it collides with the pore walls in a dynamic process that results in new vibrational excited states that are absent in the evacuated phase. Given that a nearest neighbor-type hopping mechanism was attributed to the conductivity in the evacuated framework, the charge hopping process in the guest-occupied material may likewise be described as charge carriers traversing a thermal barrier. Microscopically, the hopping probability for a charge carrier, $P$ , is proportional to the attempt frequency, $\nu$ , for a charge hopping a single barrier (Equation 1).54
$$
P=\upsilon e^{\frac{-E_{\mathrm{A}}}{k_{\mathrm{B}}}}
$$
