As observed for the conductivity isotherms, the chemiresistive effect for $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ in the presence of cis-2- butene is clearly different from the other adsorbates, even when estimating the sensitivity in the low-loading regime. This unique result may originate from the adsorbed molecule exhibiting fewer degrees of freedom—effectively a lower specific heat capacity—than cis-2-butene expresses in the gas phase. The plateau at higher loadings in Figure 5 is not attributed to a further reduction in heat capacity, but rather is likely competing adsorption of additional cis-2-butene on the crystallite surface, as supported by the gas adsorption isotherm.
Kinetics Analysis of Adsorption and Conductance upon Gas Dosing. In the case of ethane, the quantity of adsorbed gas and the change in conductivity were measured as a function of time, enabling a kinetics analysis of the chemiresistive effect to be compared to the equilibration of the bulk gas-powder mixture. An exemplary equilibration curve is shown in Figure 6 for the dosing of ethane from 19 to 45 mbar. The observed rate of adsorption suggests a combination of rate limiting effects: over very short times (less than 1 min), gas adsorption is limited by diffusion and gas conduction through the manifold, while over longer time intervals, the rate of adsorption corresponds best with an equilibration-based kinetics model for the reversible adsorption-desorption process (Figure S42).57 In contrast to the equilibration of the bulk composition, the cell conductance appears to equilibrate on a shorter time scale. In kinetics were masked in large part by gas conduction through the manifold in the first few seconds after dosing, an observation supported by the gas conduction-induced echo effect occurring in the first ${\sim}30~\mathrm{s}$ . Over the entire equilibration interval, the conductivity consistently saturated more rapidly than the bulk guest adsorption for all measured steps in pressure.
One possible explanation for the different equilibration behavior is that conductivity in the pressed pellet is likely limited by intercrystallite contact resistances, an effect broadly associated with pressed pellet conductivity measurements.58 Conduction through a single crystal is expected to be much higher than between crystals. As such, a small relative decrease in resistance at the crystallite contacts may cause a disproportionately large change in the resistance of the samples, while a comparable relative decrease in resistance within a single crystal would be largely masked. A similar rationale has been offered to explain the mechanism of chemiresistance  in sintered metal–oxide materials, where the conductivity of grain boundaries and interparticle contacts are believed to be most sensitive to gas binding.59
## CONCLUSIONS
The foregoing results show the conductivity of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ to be remarkably sensitive to solvation state. In particular, the acetonitrile-solvated material $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]{\cdot}n\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ exhibits a conductivity that is 200 times less than that of the evacuated material. In addition, $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ exhibits impressive adsorption of the gaseous hydrocarbons ethane, ethylene, acetylene, propane, propylene, and cis-2-butene, which motivated our investigation of the charge transport properties of the material upon gas adsorption. It was demonstrated that $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ indeed exhibits significant changes in conductivity dependent on hydrocarbon identity, rendering it an intriguing case study for the development of metal–organic frameworks for use in next-generation chemiresistive sensors. Here, the change in $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ conductance was found to be independent of binding strength in the case of all gases tested, and rather, the conductivity increases linearly only with the quantity of gas adsorbed in the framework. Ultimately, the sensitivity of conductance in $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ guest insertion correlates strongly with the gas-phase specific heat capacity of the adsorbate. We note that such a contribution is likely general to all materials that display a physisorption-based chemiresistivity. In the case of $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ , the largest adsorbate—cis-2-butene—violates the observed trend with molar heat capacity, consistent with a possible nanoconfinement effect. The conductivity trends were further investigated using electronic structure calculations for the pristine $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ and $\mathrm{Cu}[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{pdt})_{2}]$ -adsorbate complexes, and found to be consistent with a hopping based transport model. Finally, initial kinetics analysis of the chemiresistive and adsorption processes indicate grain and interparticle contacts are transport-limiting, and sensor performance may therefore be further optimized through nanostructuring.
More generally, we present an alternative direction for the development of new chemiresistive sensor materials focused on bulk gas adsorption properties, rather than the high temperature reactivity of surface modified composites. Given the unrivaled gas adsorption and separation properties of metal–organic frameworks at large, this class of materials may be poised for implementation in powerful new gas sensor devices. Ongoing work in this area would do well to include a focus on the chemiresistive response of protic and redox-active adsorbates to determine more broadly the factors influencing the relative chemiresisitivities in a framework with different adsorbates.
